1721
01:24:08.640 --> 01:24:18.720
I don't see you doing like a handstand and like taking like a grenade out of your back pocket and throwing it through a window as you flip off a table.

1722
01:24:20.040 --> 01:24:20.560
Okay.

1723
01:24:21.680 --> 01:24:25.960
You clearly have strong feelings about my limitations.

1724
01:24:26.420 --> 01:24:27.120
You know what?

1725
01:24:27.300 --> 01:24:31.940
I traveled with you with a guitar, and I saw what it was like.

1726
01:24:32.300 --> 01:24:36.460
You navigating a guitar through an airport, getting it on a plane.

1727
01:24:36.460 --> 01:24:38.380
It was very difficult for you.

1728
01:24:39.580 --> 01:24:40.340
All right.

1729
01:24:40.940 --> 01:24:41.840
Fair enough.

1730
01:24:42.100 --> 01:24:42.820
Fair enough.

1731
01:24:43.000 --> 01:24:44.200
I will say this.

1732
01:24:44.200 --> 01:24:49.620
I did recently purchase, I'm so excited to get it, a portable tap dancing floor.

1733
01:24:49.620 --> 01:24:50.840
This is who you are?

1734
01:24:51.540 --> 01:24:51.940
Yes.

1735
01:24:54.220 --> 01:24:55.560
So get ready for that.

1736
01:24:55.740 --> 01:24:59.240
I've already started learning a tap dance to the song Footloose.

1737
01:24:59.840 --> 01:25:05.840
And I'm going to send you a video I'm going to put on my overalls, and I'm going to dance in front of my barn on my portable floor.

1738
01:25:05.840 --> 01:25:11.640
And I'm going to come and sit at the end of your driveway and play a few glasses of the glass harmonica.

1739
01:25:12.720 --> 01:25:13.700
All right.

1740
01:25:21.980 --> 01:25:24.460
And I'm going to tap dance my little heart out.

1741
01:25:25.180 --> 01:25:27.120
Guys, that's Ben Franklin.

1742
01:25:27.680 --> 01:25:32.280
Before we go, we have a final audio clip from Andy Daly.

1743
01:25:32.460 --> 01:25:33.220
I was curious.

1744
01:25:33.220 --> 01:25:38.300
I had to know, has he ever played any other historic figures or founding fathers?

1745
01:25:39.080 --> 01:25:40.320
Here's what he had to say.

1746
01:25:40.320 --> 01:25:44.760
I did play Teddy Roosevelt in an episode of Drunk History that was very memorable.

1747
01:25:44.940 --> 01:25:49.760
I got to ride a horse and wear a fake mustache, and that was great.

1748
01:25:50.160 --> 01:25:57.480
But, you know, a long time ago, back in, I would say, 2002, I put up a sketch show at the UCB Theater in New York.

1749
01:25:57.620 --> 01:26:06.380
And one of the sketches was about Alexander Hamilton debating federalism with someone, which sounds like a terrible comedy sketch.

1750
01:26:06.500 --> 01:26:07.280
And maybe it was.

1751
01:26:07.280 --> 01:26:18.300
But I played Alexander Hamilton in that, and it is my opinion that Lin-Manuel Miranda saw that sketch and was inspired to write Hamilton.

1752
01:26:18.300 --> 01:26:21.320
And I've always felt kind of ripped off.

1753
01:26:21.520 --> 01:26:30.980
Like, you know, it's nice that Ron Chernow, who wrote the Hamilton biography, gets credit for the extent to which he inspired that play.

1754
01:26:30.980 --> 01:26:31.640
But I am not.

1755
01:26:31.800 --> 01:26:36.300
You will never see my name in the playbill or in the credits of the movie.

1756
01:26:36.620 --> 01:26:40.280
I am just very conveniently completely forgotten.

1757
01:26:40.560 --> 01:26:45.000
And I can't prove that Lin-Manuel Miranda was in the audience or ever saw that sketch.

1758
01:26:45.640 --> 01:26:50.520
But, you know, common sense dictates that that's how Hamilton came about.

1759
01:26:51.360 --> 01:26:52.380
So, but that's it.

1760
01:26:52.720 --> 01:27:00.100
Teddy Roosevelt, Alexander Hamilton, and Ben Franklin are the only founding fathers I have ever played in my entire career.

1761
01:27:01.280 --> 01:27:01.460
Wow.

1762
01:27:02.080 --> 01:27:04.020
Three more founding fathers than I've played.

1763
01:27:04.180 --> 01:27:04.920
I love it.

1764
01:27:05.060 --> 01:27:06.020
I love Andy Daly.

1765
01:27:06.020 --> 01:27:15.700
You guys, if you want to keep listening to this very, very, very funny person, you can find him on his own Earwolf podcast called Bonanas for Bonanza,

1766
01:27:16.020 --> 01:27:20.920
which is a rewatch podcast of the classic Western TV show Bonanza.

1767
01:27:21.500 --> 01:27:26.060
You can also find him on Twitter and Instagram at TV's Andy Daly.

1768
01:27:26.300 --> 01:27:28.720
His last name is spelled D-A-L-Y.

1769
01:27:28.980 --> 01:27:30.860
I cannot wait to listen to that podcaSt.

1770
01:27:30.860 --> 01:27:32.880
I used to watch Bonanza with my dad.

1771
01:27:33.340 --> 01:27:34.380
It's Andy Daly.

1772
01:27:34.500 --> 01:27:35.260
That's all you need to know.

1773
01:27:35.260 --> 01:27:35.820
All right.

1774
